Id: pam001w
Severity: WARN
Category: accounts
Check: check_pam
Fix: Install libpam-pwquality (libpwquality) and set minlen = 14 in pwquality.conf.
References: pam_pwquality(8), pwquality.conf(5)
Controls: CIS 5.2; NIST IA-5(1); ISO 5.17, 8.5; CE User access control

No PAM module checks the quality of new passwords: none of
pam_pwquality, pam_cracklib or pam_passwdqc is in a password stack in
/etc/pam.d (or /usr/lib/pam.d). Users can then set passwords that are
short, a dictionary word or their user name, and those fall to a
guessing attack first. (pam_unix's obscure option, Debian's default,
makes only a few weak checks.)

To fix it, install libpam-pwquality on Debian and Ubuntu (it adds
itself to common-password) or libpwquality on Fedora, RHEL and SUSE,
and set its rules in /etc/security/pwquality.conf: minlen = 14 is the
CIS benchmarks' figure.
